  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nRelatively low amplitude longwave ridging will allow a series \nof shortwaves to permeate east along the top of the ridge \nthrough early this coming work week bringing above average \nmoisture to the region for the 6-10 day period. With these \nshortwaves will come severe chances (mainly Sunday) in the form \nof wind and hail. Beyond these threats the rains will certainly \nbe welcome and beneficial to easing the worsening drought though\nis unfortunately too little, too late for many crops. It may \neven put a short stop to the ongoing wheat/sugar beet harvest. \nDry/hot ridging does briefly return for the middle of next week \nbefore we transition to prevailing SW flow aloft indicating \nanother wet period, though uncertainty remains on any amounts or\nassociated hazards (flooding/severe) for the late week.\n\n- Today\n\nTrends have largely backed off on the severe potential for \ntonight across south central North Dakota. Still could see some \nhailers across areas from Bismarck to Jamestown but most severe \nactivity looks to stay in South Dakota tonight. Therefore wont \ndive into the forecast too much for tonight given the lessening \nthreat for impacts.\n\n- Sunday \n\nCAMS along with machine learning guidance continues to support \na severe risk across North Dakota and possibly Minnesota on \nSunday. As an increasingly negatively tilted upper low ejects \neast from interior Washington state into southeast Alberta by \n00z Monday a 60kt+ 500mb maxima will be moving across the MT/ND \nborder providing the forcing to initiate storms along the higher\nterrain. Increasing flow at 850-700mb of 30-45kts will support \nelongating low level shear and MUCAPE of 2000-3000 J/kg should \nprovide plenty of instability to sustain and east moving MCS. \nWeak LCL-EL shear should limit cold pool growth along with a \nrelative lack of dry air through the profile given PWATs \nexceeding 1.6\". Overall think winds of 60- 70mph are entirely \nlikely with an outside chance at a few sig gusts over 75mph. \nWhile wind is an obvious threat with the potential MCS tornadoes\nalso cannot be ruled out, however SBCIN approaching -100 j/kg \nand 0-3km shear of only 20-25kts indicate mesovortex potential \ncould be limited. Hail, unless MCS is not the dominant mode, \nshould be limited to quarter size at most once the complex gets \ninto our area. Given the MUCAPE pool generally remaining along \nand south of HWY 200 would think the highest threat for sig \nwinds will remain more along the I94 corridor but cannot rule \nout severe (60+ mph) winds all the way to the intl border.\n\n- Monday Wind\n\nAs the upper low lifts northeast through Lake Manitoba and Lake\nWinnipeg strong W/SW winds will wrap around the southern \nperiphery of the low. EFI does pick up on this nicely with some \nshading over the 0.8 threshold that seems to correspond nicely \nwith wind advisory criteria for us historically, anecdotally \nspeaking. Given a modest BL depth to around 800mb and flow \nwithin that layer of 30-40kts and wind advisory criteria with \nsustained speeds over 25kts or gusts over 39kts will be in play.\nGiven RH that seems to want to bottom out in the mid to upper \n30s fire weather conditions do not currently seem to be of \nparticular concern but may be something to dive into a later \ntime.\n\n- Work Week\n\nNear zonal to southwesterly flow aloft continues into next \nweek. Temps look to be above average with several chances for \nprecipitation as weak shortwaves come through. Machine learning \ndoes indicate increased chances for severe weather but with a \nlarge spatial spread day to day lowering the confidence at the \nthis time in impacts. Overall seasonal moisture and increased \nshear from the passing shortwaves amid SW flow do conceptually \nsupport the risk for strong to severe storms mainly Thursday \ninto Saturday.",
